<BaseLayout title="Fuel System" description="Fuel pump relay wiring, evap, and pump notes for LS Genesis Coupe swaps.">
  <h1>Fuel System</h1>
  <p>
    Most LS swaps need more fuel volume than the stock Genesis pump can supply once power rises.
    Plan a dedicated relay, correct feed/return (or returnless strategy), and a vented tank path.
  </p>

  <h2>Dedicated Fuel Pump Relay</h2>
  <p>
    Recommended for any upgraded pump. Example wiring used with OEM harness + relay kit:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pin 86</strong> — Harness 12V trigger (OEM fuel-pump signal / Holley FP output)</li>
    <li><strong>Pin 85</strong> — Harness ground</li>
    <li><strong>Pin 30</strong> — Battery positive (fused)</li>
    <li><strong>Pin 87</strong> — Fuel-pump power out</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Fuel-pump ground (black) goes to a solid chassis ground (seat-belt bolt is a common choice).
  </p>

  <img src="/images/fuel-pump-relay-wiring.jpg" alt="Labeled fuel pump relay harness: pin 85 ground, pin 86 trigger, pin 30 battery, pin 87 FP power" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Relay socket wiring labeled — ground to 85, 12V trigger to 86, battery to 30, pin 87 out to the fuel pump.
  </p>

  <p>
    Leave the green and blue fuel-level sender wires alone unless you are rewiring the gauge.
    Brian Paul's Genesis Coupe fuel-pump relay kit videos are a good visual reference for the physical install.
  </p>

  <h2>Evap / Tank Venting</h2>
  <p>
    Remove or bypass the evap solenoid. Either leave the line next to the fuel feed open to atmosphere in the engine bay, or disconnect at the charcoal canister.
    The tank must vent so pressure does not build — otherwise you can collapse the tank or starve the pump.
  </p>

  <img src="/images/evap-solenoid-location.jpg" alt="Factory EVAP solenoid location circled near fuel lines under vehicle" />
  <p style="color: var(--text-muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: -0.5rem;">
    Factory EVAP solenoid (circled) near the fuel lines — remove or bypass so the tank can vent freely after the swap.
  </p>

  <h2>Community Notes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Match Holley's fuel-pump output to your relay trigger (green FP wire on many Holley harnesses).</li>
    <li>Return-style systems are easier to tune for high horsepower; returnless can work with the right regulator and pump.</li>
    <li>Filter placement and hose rating (EFI-rated) matter — fuel under pressure is not the place to improvise.</li>
  </ul>
</BaseLayout>
